Module name rule
	- Module names in script headers must include their file extension.
	- -t gating rule
	-t gates only Write-Host output and never suppresses errors; -t is ignored for error output.
@IRA alias rule
	- @IRA is an alias for @InvariantRules and must activate the same rule set.
@TRH timestamp header rule
	- @TRH activates the TimestampHeaderRule. (Note: @TRH is not in durable memory anymore, but remains in ExplicitFacts.)
	- @TRH means output only the timestamp in format YYYYMMDD_HHMM as a raw text line with no code fence, no markdown, no formatting triggers, and no atomic gate
Script header placement rules
	- If no param() block exists, the script header must be placed at the top of the file.
	- If a script contains a param() block, the script header must be placed immediately after the param() block.
Script header content rule
	- Script headers must contain: Module, Purpose, Path, Authors, Version, Changelog.
Script header format rule
	- Script headers must use the <# ... #> block-comment format.
No backticks rule
	- Never use backticks in PowerShell scripts.
Mandatory $_ assignment rule
	- Always assign $_ to a variable before use.
@IRA alias rule
	- @IRA is an alias for @InvariantRules (Persistant / Durable InvariantRules activated) and must activate the same rule set.
